% ============================================================================
% brain-parts — a major brain part → the primary function it controls
% (adj-facts-stdlib, ANATOMY).
% ============================================================================
% A middle/high-school neuroanatomy fact set on the way to medicine: the brain
% is not one undivided organ — different PARTS do different JOBS. The cerebrum
% (the big wrinkled top) does conscious thought; the cerebellum (the little
% "cauliflower" at the back) coordinates movement and balance; the brainstem
% (the stalk into the spinal cord) runs the automatic life-support functions
% like breathing; the hypothalamus is a tiny control knob for drives such as
% hunger; the thalamus is the switchboard that relays sensation up to the
% cerebrum; and the hippocampus lays down memory. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? brain_part_function(cerebellum, $Job)   % coordination_of_voluntary_movement
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `brain_part_function(part, function)` carrying THAT ROW'S
% OWN citation (RS-5e — before #14986 it was the table's single envelope),
% so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% function WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on
% anything that is not one of these parts (it never invents a job).
%
% The parts, as a little truth table:
%
%     brain_part      function                            in one phrase
%     -------------   ---------------------------------   ------------------------
%     cerebrum        conscious_activity                  conscious thought
%     cerebellum      coordination_of_voluntary_movement  smooth, balanced movement
%     brainstem       breathing                           automatic life support
%     brainstem       temperature_regulation              also autonomic (added this cycle)
%     brainstem       respiration                         also autonomic (added this cycle)
%     brainstem       heart_rate                          also autonomic (added this cycle)
%     brainstem       wake_sleep_cycles                   also autonomic (added this cycle)
%     brainstem       coughing                            also autonomic (added this cycle)
%     brainstem       sneezing                            also autonomic (added this cycle)
%     brainstem       digestion                           also autonomic (added this cycle)
%     brainstem       vomiting                            also autonomic (added this cycle)
%     brainstem       swallowing                          also autonomic (added this cycle)
%     hypothalamus    hunger                              drives (hunger/thirst/sleep)
%     thalamus        relays                              sensory switchboard
%     hippocampus     memory                              laying down memory
%
% EXTENDED this cycle from 6 to 15 rows: `brainstem`'s own cited source
% sentence always listed TEN autonomic functions ("breathing, temperature
% regulation, respiration, heart rate, wake-sleep cycles, coughing,
% sneezing, digestion, vomiting, and swallowing"), but only the first,
% `breathing`, had ever been turned into a row. This mirrors the
% many-valued-per-key shape `kingdoms.adj`/`opposites.adj`/`synonyms.adj`
% already established: each additional function is a NEW row sharing the
% same `brainstem` key, so the original `row (brainstem, breathing)` is
% untouched and every new row is a pure addition. WebFetch re-verified the
% brainstem sentence live before writing -- byte-identical to what the
% header already quoted. `respiration` is kept distinct from `breathing`
% because the source lists them as two separate items in its own comma
% list, not because this table asserts they mean something different.
%
% Each `function` value is a single lowercase token/phrase that appears VERBATIM
% in the cited source sentence (see the provenance block), so the recall is a
% faithful echo of the source, not a paraphrase. The query also runs BACKWARD —
% bind a function and recall the part that performs it:
%
%     ? brain_part_function($Part, breathing)   % brainstem — reverse recall
%
% Something that is not one of these parts — a neuron, the skull, the spinal
% cord — has no row, so a recall on it ABSTAINS rather than inventing a job.
% That honest-abstention property is what makes the table safe to reason from:
% a medicine agent reasons UP from "which part does what" before it can reason
% about a stroke localized to the cerebellum or a brainstem bleed.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance. EVERY ROW CARRIES ITS OWN SPAN AND ITS OWN LOCATOR, below, in the
% row's `{ ... }` block (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e) — read those, not a restatement here.
% Three pages, all primary U.S. government references (NCI SEER Training and two
% NIH/NLM StatPearls chapters), so `trust authoritative`.
%
% WHAT #14986 CHANGED, and it is the sharpest mis-citation in this cascade after
% `biology/skeleton-bones.adj`. The CEREBRUM sentence was this table's `source`
% — the field that carries the tier — for ALL FIFTEEN rows, on a SEER page
% that, measured:
%
%   * contains NO brain-stem function at all (its only brain stem sentence is
%     anatomical: "continuous with the spinal cord and comprising the medulla
%     oblongata, pons, midbrain, and parts of the hypothalamus");
%   * contains NO thalamus sentence — the string "thalamus" occurs there only
%     inside "hypothalamus";
%   * contains the word "hippocampus" ZERO times.
%
% So thirteen of the fifteen rows were warranted by a sentence from a page that
% cannot support them. Their real evidence was already in the file, one line
% below, as untiered `cites` on the two StatPearls chapters.
%
% THE ENVELOPE IS NOW A FRAMING SPAN: "The 3 main parts of the human brain are
% the cerebrum, cerebellum, and brainstem." It names three parts and NO
% function, so it warrants none of the fifteen (part, function) rows.
%
% TEN ROWS SHARE THE BRAINSTEM SENTENCE, which lists ten autonomic functions.
% Each row carries its own copy and each is pinned individually. (A draft of
% this header said NINE, 52 lines below a line of this same file that already
% said TEN.)
%
% ONE SPAN IS NOT SUBJECT-INITIAL, and that is fine: the thalamus row's warrant
% is "Sensory neurons bring sensory input from the body to the thalamus, which
% relays this information to the cerebrum." Its grammatical subject is the
% neurons, not the thalamus — but it states the relation the row asserts, which
% is what a warrant has to do. A pairing script that required subject position
% rejected it, and the script was wrong, not the sentence.
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table brain_part_function {
    columns brain_part, function

    row (cerebrum, conscious_activity) {
        % This sentence was the table's envelope, so it warranted all
        % fifteen rows -- including thirteen whose subject its page
        % never mentions.
        source "The cerebrum is the part of the brain that receives and processes conscious sensation, generates thought, and controls conscious activity."
        locator "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/brain/tumors/anatomy/brain.html"
    }
    row (cerebellum, coordination_of_voluntary_movement) {
        source "The cerebellum controls the coordination of voluntary movement and receives sensory information from the brain and spinal cord to fine-tune the precision and accuracy of motor activity."
        locator "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551718/"
    }
    row (brainstem, breathing) {
        % TEN rows share this one sentence: it lists ten autonomic
        % functions. Each row carries its own copy so any one can be
        % checked, or broken, alone.
        %
        % The SEER page in the old envelope has no brain-stem FUNCTION
        % at all -- its only brain stem sentence is anatomical. This
        % sentence, from StatPearls, was a table-level `cites`.
        source "The brainstem houses the principal centers that perform autonomic functions such as breathing, temperature regulation, respiration, heart rate, wake-sleep cycles, coughing, sneezing, digestion, vomiting, and swallowing."
        locator "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551718/"
    }
    row (brainstem, temperature_regulation) {
        source "The brainstem houses the principal centers that perform autonomic functions such as breathing, temperature regulation, respiration, heart rate, wake-sleep cycles, coughing, sneezing, digestion, vomiting, and swallowing."
        locator "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551718/"
    }
    row (brainstem, respiration) {
        source "The brainstem houses the principal centers that perform autonomic functions such as breathing, temperature regulation, respiration, heart rate, wake-sleep cycles, coughing, sneezing, digestion, vomiting, and swallowing."
        locator "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551718/"
    }
    row (brainstem, heart_rate) {
        source "The brainstem houses the principal centers that perform autonomic functions such as breathing, temperature regulation, respiration, heart rate, wake-sleep cycles, coughing, sneezing, digestion, vomiting, and swallowing."
        locator "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551718/"
    }
    row (brainstem, wake_sleep_cycles) {
        source "The brainstem houses the principal centers that perform autonomic functions such as breathing, temperature regulation, respiration, heart rate, wake-sleep cycles, coughing, sneezing, digestion, vomiting, and swallowing."
        locator "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551718/"
    }
    row (brainstem, coughing) {
        source "The brainstem houses the principal centers that perform autonomic functions such as breathing, temperature regulation, respiration, heart rate, wake-sleep cycles, coughing, sneezing, digestion, vomiting, and swallowing."
        locator "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551718/"
    }
    row (brainstem, sneezing) {
        source "The brainstem houses the principal centers that perform autonomic functions such as breathing, temperature regulation, respiration, heart rate, wake-sleep cycles, coughing, sneezing, digestion, vomiting, and swallowing."
        locator "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551718/"
    }
    row (brainstem, digestion) {
        source "The brainstem houses the principal centers that perform autonomic functions such as breathing, temperature regulation, respiration, heart rate, wake-sleep cycles, coughing, sneezing, digestion, vomiting, and swallowing."
        locator "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551718/"
    }
    row (brainstem, vomiting) {
        source "The brainstem houses the principal centers that perform autonomic functions such as breathing, temperature regulation, respiration, heart rate, wake-sleep cycles, coughing, sneezing, digestion, vomiting, and swallowing."
        locator "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551718/"
    }
    row (brainstem, swallowing) {
        source "The brainstem houses the principal centers that perform autonomic functions such as breathing, temperature regulation, respiration, heart rate, wake-sleep cycles, coughing, sneezing, digestion, vomiting, and swallowing."
        locator "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551718/"
    }
    row (hypothalamus, hunger) {
        source "The hypothalamus controls hunger, thirst, and sleep."
        locator "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551718/"
    }
    row (thalamus, relays) {
        % PROMOTED from a table-level `cites`. On the old envelope's
        % SEER page the string "thalamus" occurs only inside
        % "hypothalamus"; there is no thalamus sentence there.
        source "Sensory neurons bring sensory input from the body to the thalamus, which relays this information to the cerebrum."
        locator "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551718/"
    }
    row (hippocampus, memory) {
        % PROMOTED from a table-level `cites`. The word "hippocampus"
        % occurs ZERO times on the old envelope's SEER page.
        source "The hippocampus is the \"flash drive\" of the human brain and is often associated with memory consolidation and decision-making, but it is far more complex in structure and function than a flash drive."
        locator "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482171/"
    }

    % The envelope is the FRAMING span -- the page's one-sentence
    % statement of what the brain is made OF. It names three parts and
    % NO function, so it warrants none of the fifteen (part, function)
    % rows. Until #14986 the envelope was the CEREBRUM row's own
    % sentence, standing as the tiered warrant for fourteen rows it
    % does not state -- thirteen of them from a page that never
    % mentions their subject at all.
    source "The 3 main parts of the human brain are the cerebrum, cerebellum, and brainstem."
    locator "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551718/"
    trust authoritative
}
